    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d621">

                <pb facs="0731" n="721" sameAs="frus:frus1942China/pg721"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.42793/773: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-07-29T12:00:00+07:00">July 29,
                            1942—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 6:05 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>880. Embassy’s 767, June 29, 9 a.m.<anchor n="21" xml:id="d621-f1"/> Foreign
                    Office note July 25 requests there be added to list of experts to be sent to
                    China by the Department an adviser to the Chinese industrial co-operatives.
                    Latter express desire for services of Professor Mackenzie Stevens of the
                    University of Maryland to remain at least three months. Note also requests that
                    two assistants accompany Stevens to remain for one year.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d621-f1" n="21" xml:id="d621-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed, but see despatch No. 490, June 29, from the Ambassador in China,
                        <ref target="#pg711">p. 711</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
